── WHAT A FRESH SESSION MUST KNOW ──
1. SYNC IS WORSE THAN OLD NOTES SAY, and the instrument was why. Every sync number quoted before
   2026-08-23 was ~31 ms optimistic: the oracle carried an exact +18.00 ms detection bias (half a
   30 fps frame + half a 128-sample envelope window) and never measured the VIDEO reference at all
   (13.5 ms) while correcting the audio one. CI now gates the SYMMETRIC metric at ≤90 ms and reads
   40-66 ms. The instrument's own noise floor is ~8-9 ms 1σ — no band below that is measurable.
   O4's job is to bring this to ≤20 ms; re-tighten the band then.
2. THE AUDIO ANCHOR CANNOT SEE INPUT LATENCY. It dates sample 0 from when a worklet batch ARRIVES,
   so device/stream buffering is invisible and audio lands late. Loopback-measured at +128.7 ms
   (sd 0.70); dating from the audio clock instead changes it by 1.1 ms, so this is signal-path, not
   messaging. Production now subtracts the platform-reported track latency (bounded, logged, never a
   fitted constant — a 90 ms fallback was rejected on those grounds in 2026-07).
3. NOTHING IN ARMING MAY AWAIT WITHOUT A DEADLINE. Each step was bounded but the joins were not, and
   that froze recording with a stale "Waiting for microphone…" label. Budgets now bound the joins,
   every durable-worker round trip has a deadline, the record button cancels an arm, and a pagehide
   guard releases devices. Keep it that way.
4. EVIDENCE IS BROWSER-SIDE. Unit tests cover pure logic only; anything about capture, export,
   memory or bundles must be measured in a real browser on the PRODUCTION build. QA on the prod-build
   port only, never the dev server.
5. THE TAIL IS A DRAIN PROBLEM, AND MediaRecorder LIES ABOUT BEING IDLE. The shipped composite used to
   lose 2734 ms off a 4K take; it now loses 43-320 ms, because stop() kills the painting and then
   PROBES the encoder with requestData() until it answers empty. Waiting for the byte flow to go quiet
   instead looks equivalent and is not — under load this recorder emits four chunks in ten seconds, so
   silence is the normal state and means nothing. Anything that stops a MediaRecorder in this codebase
   should assume the same. The band now runs where it can fail: `npm run oracle:load` (HEAVY, two
   phases). THE RAW HALF IS ALSO FIXED (P0-tail-raw) AND ITS LESSON IS THE OPPOSITE ONE: you cannot
   drain a raw channel by ending its track, because Chrome stops a MediaRecorder whose stream has
   gone inactive and takes the backlog with it. Starve the source instead — 1 fps, same frame size —
   and drain. Do not write a new tail claim without running `npm run exp -- o4step2` and
   `npm run exp -- p0tailraw`.
6. THERE ARE TWO LIVE-COMPOSITE ENGINES, AND v2 IS THE DEFAULT (flipped 2026-08-24). v2 = worker +
   WebCodecs + WebGL2 + fMP4 to its own SyncAccessHandle. v1 (MediaRecorder) is the LADDER under
   it, all three rungs real: capability (no TrackProcessor/AudioEncoder → v1 from the start, which
   is all of Apple WebKit and Firefox), start failure (v2 throws → v1 takes the take), mid-take
   watchdog (sustained <12 fps after first output → composite refused, take renders from raw
   channels). `?engine=v1` / localStorage force the old engine. HISTORY THAT WILL SAVE YOU A WEEK:
   three sessions of "WebCodecs delivers 2-10 fps" were the INSTRUMENT — a fresh throwaway
   profile's FIRST VideoEncoder pays a multi-second init (PER LAUNCH, so production prewarms at
   mount: prearm.ts → encoderWarm.ts), every rig take fit inside it, and the old watchdog killed
   the take mid-init. Warm, v2 beats v1 on every measured axis (28.4 vs 29.3 fps; main thread 0 vs
   198 ms; CPU peak 127 vs 196 %; oracle sync 33-48 vs ~60 ms; drains its own encoder). o4step2
   warms before measuring ({"cold":true} measures the transient on purpose); the watchdog clock
   starts at first output (capture/compositorWatchdog.ts). Anything touching capture/* keeps BOTH
   engines green: `npm run oracle` (v2 by default) AND `node scripts/oracle.mjs --engine=v1`.
7. THE EDIT IS PERSISTED NOW (F4, editsRepo, IndexedDB v2). Before that a refresh silently threw away
   every trim and cut while faithfully restoring the recording. If you add a field to EditState, it is
   automatically persisted — make sure it survives a round trip through structured clone.
8. THE COMPOSITE'S GEOMETRY LIVES IN THREE PLACES and they must agree, because an unedited export
   packet-copies the composite: compose/layout.ts (render), capture/compositor.worker.ts (v2 live) and
   liveComposite.ts (v1 live). timeline/cameraTrack.ts owns the PiP rect for the first two; a unit test
   pins the default pose against the editor's historical CSS numbers.
9. ANYTHING THAT CHANGES PIXELS IS AN EDIT, AND MUST SAY SO. isDefaultEdit is what decides whether the
   export may packet-copy the composite, so every new visual feature has to make it false: F4's camera
   track, F3's background frame and F2's viewport all do. The other half of the same rule is that an
   INACTIVE style must be dropped rather than stored (clampEditState deletes a background that paints
   and insets nothing), or an untouched take silently loses the instant path.
10. THE RIG IS WRONG BEFORE THE PRODUCT IS — three times in one day. A 450 px zoom "error" was an
   expectation that had not been clipped to the canvas. A 9851 ms "tail gap" was a measurement lane
   started at stop instead of at the beginning. A 16.2 fps "config catastrophe" was the first row of a
   matrix paying for cold start. Before reporting an alarming number, check the instrument: add the
   warm-up, print the intermediate quantities, and make the rig prove itself on a case whose answer
   you already know.
11. THE ONE-BRANCH-PER-TASK RULE COLLIDES WITH THE AUTO-COMMIT HOOK. A Stop hook runs
   `git add -A && git commit && git push origin HEAD:main` after every turn, so work is on main
   before a session can open its own branch, and a branch left checked out at the end would be pushed
   TO main. Sessions that cannot merge inside one turn should work on main and carry the reasoning in
   the commit message (see the F3 record commit for the shape).

13. THE EXPORT'S FLOOR IS DECODE, NOT SCHEDULING — measured, and it retires a whole family of ideas.
   O5 assumed the render loop was badly overlapped. It is not: mediabunny's encoder wrapper already
   keeps 4 frames in the VideoEncoder queue, and the expensive part of "decode" is JS on the same
   thread, so it cannot hide behind the draw either. 12 s 1080p edited take: decode-wait 1295 ms,
   draw 450 ms, encode-wait 28 ms — against 962 ms to decode the same frames with no draw and no
   encode at all. An encode lookahead window AND a decoder prefetch pump were both built, measured,
   and deleted for buying nothing. Do not propose "pipeline the export" again without first running
   `npm run exp -- o5` and beating the decode floor it prints. The way past the floor is to decode
   FEWER frames, which is smart cut.
   Two traps in the same area, both paid for already: (a) the FIRST lane of any export matrix pays the
   codec cold start — warm with a discarded export and run both orders, or you will report the second
   engine as faster whichever one it is; (b) compose/scratch.ts deletes the previous finished export
   when a new one finishes, so an earlier ExportResult.blob goes dead and reads as a bare
   `TypeError: network error`. A rig comparing several files must copy each one into memory first.

14. EVERY SYNC NUMBER THIS PROJECT QUOTED BEFORE 2026-08-24 WAS THE RENDER'S, and the render is the
   path a user gets LAST. The oracle exported by calling exportRecording directly, so the instant
   packet copy (what an unedited take takes) and smart cut were invisible to it. They are not any
   more: the rig records a composite, the trim goes through compose/choose.ts — the product's own
   ladder — and every oracle line now prints `inst=` (instant-path sync), `trim=<path>/<sync>` and
   `compT0=` (where the composite's clock starts). The first honest run found the instant path
   outside the band. If you change anything in compose/*, read those three fields, not just `sync=`.

12. THE UI RIG SERVES dist/, AND DOES NOT BUILD IT. `scripts/ui-shot.mjs` runs `vite preview`, so a
   flow that "does not work" may simply be yesterday's bundle. Run `npm run build` first. The same
   file is also where a synthetic drag goes wrong: `startDrag` listens on the ELEMENT and leans on
   pointer capture, which a synthetic pointer id never gets, so pointermove has to be dispatched on
   the element rather than the window.

