again — and no single correction can fix a sign flip, which kills the whole short-window-plus-
correction family rather than one member of it. (On screen the shape is worth naming: the encoder
spends 11-19 KB on each of the ~7 deltas after a keyframe, then collapses to ~200 B once the picture
holds still. The warm-up proves that is the ENCODER settling, not the content starting.)
So the window became the GOP — one window, 150 frames, exactly what the shipped 5 s cadence produces.
Scored on both rigs: screen −6.7 / −7.3 / −12.9 / −15.4 % (was −7 to −43), motion −39.9 / −36.2 /
−31.2 / −30.5 % (was −59 to −69). SCREEN IS INSIDE THE ±20 % GATE FOR THE FIRST TIME; motion is not,
so it is not shipped and the panel keeps F7's estimate. Probe cost went 1.7-2.5 s → 5.6-6.0 s.
ATTEMPT 5 HAS A COUNTABLE QUESTION, NOT A MODELLING ONE: motion is a UNIFORM 1.44× under at every
step, including 1080p where the probe draws the composed frame 1:1 and rescaling cannot be blamed.
One number, not a curve, means something countable is missing — a keyframe the render emits and the
model does not, a first GOP costing more than a middle one, or the audio term. Count them first.
WHY IT EXISTS: F7b's per-step size is predicted from the composite, and the composite's encoder is not
the export's encoder — 0.97 vs 1.84 Mbps on the same text pixels, so the prediction lands 47 % low at
1440p. The idea was to stop predicting and measure: encode a bit of the take at each step through the
export's own encoder and price the file from that.
THREE DESIGNS, ALL MEASURED AGAINST REAL RENDERED SIZES (12 s takes, screen-like and full-motion
content, `npm run exp -- o11`, which now scores any candidate automatically):
  1. key+delta pair sampled from the COMPOSITE            +136 % / −68 %
     Wrong pixels — a composite frame carries MediaRecorder's ringing on text (expensive to re-encode)
     and is already smoothed on motion (cheap). The error flips sign with content, which is the tell.
  2. key+delta pair composed from the RAW CHANNELS through the production drawVideoFrame  +128 % / −64 %
     Right pixels, wrong shape: one delta against a fresh keyframe is not what a file is made of.
  3. two 15-frame WINDOWS of consecutive composed frames  −7 to −43 % / −59 to −69 %
     Better on screen content, still far out on motion: the measured delta (4.8 KB at 1080p) is a
     THIRD of what the same content costs the real render (14-19 KB/frame).
NOT SHIPPED, deliberately: the panel keeps F7's estimate, whose error is smaller on the content that
matters and — the point — already known and written down. A probe 60 % out on one content type is not
an improvement on a model 20-45 % out on the other. The probe cost 1.7-2.5 s, also over its budget.
WHAT ATTEMPT 4 MUST HANDLE: the unexplained factor of three between a 30-frame encode and a 360-frame
one. Rate control has not settled in the short encode; find out what settles it (longer window? a
prime-the-encoder pass? reading the achieved rate from a longer sample?) and prove it on BOTH content
types before touching the panel.
GATES (unchanged): measured size within ±20 % of the shown number on EVERY step, on BOTH rigs, twice
over · panel opens in under 100 ms with the probe still running · zero orphan scratch files after 20
opens · default step still exact and still instant.

