## AI1. export-for-ai — the button  [PO priority, medium; READY]
Why: agents don't watch video — they sample frames and pay ~1 token per 750 pixels. A recording is
useful to an agent only if it is layered so reading cost scales with descent. PO scoped v1 to
maximum token cheapness; every richer layer is AI2+ and must not creep into this task.
Goal: one "For AI" control in the export panel → a zip (INDEX at the zip root; unzipped it works
as a plain folder):
  INDEX.md (≤~150 tokens: duration, resolution, clock note, keyframe list, approx token price per
  layer — the agent budgets its descent from this file alone) · events.jsonl (v1 = capture facts
  only: channel on/off segments, stalls, duration — all already in Recording, no new capture
  surface) · keyframes/ (pixel-delta sampled; per keyframe a ≤1024 px full view + a full-res crop
  of the changed region, WebP, timestamp-in-filename in ms) · video.mp4 (the ordinary default-tier
  export, bottom layer — costs tokens only if opened).
Design rules (they are decisions, recorded in the spec): the bundle FOLLOWS THE EDIT — keyframe
and event times remapped through the same output→recording mapping the render uses, cut content
absent · all timestamps on the RECORDING EPOCH with clockOffsetMs declared in INDEX when the
video's t=0 differs (P0-instant-sync's lesson: composite time is not recording time) · INDEX is
signal-derived, never generated prose (the no-AI rule covers export-time summarization) ·
keyframe selection pure + unit-tested with a defined delta metric (share of changed pixels on a
downscaled luma diff).
Implementation notes: tap composed output frames inside the machinery the render already iterates
(compose/render.ts loop, chosen via choose.ts) rather than building a second decode pipeline —
note 13: decode is the floor, nothing may decode the take twice · a store-only zip is ~50 lines
(WebP/MP4 are already compressed) — no new dependency unless it stays tiny · write through
scratch (O1) so memory stays flat · src/core owns the bundle builder, the app owns only the button.
Constraints: instant path untouched — the mp4 inside the bundle may be the packet copy, carrying
its measured `inst=` sync defect until P0-instant-sync lands, and INDEX's clock note says so ·
no knobs in the UI · defaults of every other export path unchanged.
GATES:
· text layer priced: INDEX ≤150 tokens (chars/4) on a real take · events.jsonl ≤30 tokens/min
· keyframe economy: a 60 s mostly-static synthetic take → ≤8 keyframes · a 10 s idle span emits 0
  keyframes after its first · a motion burst concentrates keyframes inside it (distribution reported)
· correctness by the existing rig: each of the oracle take's flashes appears in a keyframe whose
  filename time is within ±150 ms of the known flash time
· edit fidelity: a take with a cut → no keyframe from the cut span, times remapped (rig proof)
· cost: bundle wall clock ≤1.5× the same take's full render (report ×)
· zip: INDEX at root · every file INDEX names exists (unit test) · opens as a plain folder
· typecheck + tests + oracle green · instant-path logs unchanged.

